<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Detailed results</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section presents detailed results on downstream tasks along with concise descriptions of the datasets.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Emotion Recognition</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FACED</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite>
We evaluate on the FACED dataset, which contains 32-channel EEG recordings (originally at 250 Hz, resampled to 200 Hz) from 123 subjects across nine emotion classes. The data is segmented into 10,332 samples of 10 seconds each. We follow the standard split: subjects 1–80 for training, 81–100 for validation, and 101–123 for testing.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A3.T8" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 8: </span>The results of different methods on emotion recognition (FACED, 9-class).</figcaption>
