<section id="A3.SS8" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.8 </span>Abnormal Detection</h3>

<div id="A3.SS8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A3.SS8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TUAB</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Obeid and Picone, 2016</a>)</cite> is used for abnormal EEG detection, where recordings are labeled as normal or abnormal. It shares the same 23-channel, 256 Hz format as TUEV. The dataset is split into 409,455 10-second samples for binary classification. We follow the provided training-test split and apply an 80%-20% training-validation split, consistent with BIOT. We resampled at 200 Hz, band-pass at 0.5-99.5 Hz, and directly used all channels and their positions. TUAB is part of the TUH dataset, which is freely available with registration required.</p>
</div>
